Z||000000||862M4inCA||08-13-2001||10:28 AM||||Well guys, I finally decided it was time for my headlights to actually run up and down without all the loud noises due to lack of lubrication and took them apart to lube and inspect the gears. At first glance inside the motors all appeared to be ok aside from a couple sheared teeth which were quickly taken care of with a flip of the gear. New grease and 45 minutes later the motor is back in the car. Now when I turn the lights on the motor runs the lights down, and when I turn them off the lights pop up (drivers side only since I still need the brush assy for the passenger side). Turning the handle in the direction indicated to go up they crank up, making me think it's a problem with the motor itself, not accidentally putting a gear in backwards. Now when I "flipped" the gear I only turned it half a turn so it contacted the good set of teeth on the opposite side. What's the deal here? Do I maybe have an electrical problem that I wasn't aware of previously since I did keep them popped up all the time and left them disconnected? I'm completely stumpt on this one guys.....please help.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Rodrv6||08-13-2001||05:39 PM||||Did you take the magnet out?  If it is in the wrong way, the motors will run backwards.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||08-15-2001||11:47 PM||||Yes, I did remove the magnet, however I thought I had made certain to orient it back in the same way I removed it from the armature. I'll check that when I have the time. Also, I have noticed that I have to give the manual wheel (to open them manually) a few turns to activate the motor....doesn't matter up or down, possible indication that I need a new brush assy? the brushes in the motor don't appear to be bad, but they do look a little on the thin side (about 1/8" or so). any ideas?||||reg||1
